{"name":"Waterloo","hex":"#7b7c94","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Waterloo","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A village in Walloon Brabant, Belgium; the site of a major military battle in 1815.","A battle fought at Waterloo, Belgium on June 18, 1815, resulting in the epic, final defeat of Napoleon Bonaparte."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Often in the term meet one's Waterloo: a notable and decisive defeat following an encounter with a powerful opponent or a problem that is too difficult."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Waterloo"}],"entries":[{"title":"Battle of Waterloo","blocks":[{"text":"The Battle of Waterloo was fought on Sunday 18 June 1815, near Waterloo, being the last engagement with Napoleon I. The French Imperial Army under the command of Napoleon I was defeated by two armies of the Seventh Coalition. One was a British-led force with units from the United Kingdom, the Netherlands, Hanover, Brunswick, and Nassau, under the command of field marshal Arthur Wellesley, Duke of Wellington. The other comprised three corps of the Prussian army under Field Marshal Gebhard Leberecht von Blücher. The battle was known contemporaneously as the Battle of Mont Saint-Jean in France and La Belle Alliance in Prussia."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Battle_of_Waterloo_1815.PNG/330px-Battle_of_Waterloo_1815.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Waterloo","wikidata":"Q48314"},{"title":"Waterloo, Belgium","blocks":[{"text":"Waterloo is a municipality in Wallonia, located in the province of Walloon Brabant, Belgium, which in 2011 had a population of 29,706 and an area of 21.03 km2 (8.12 sq mi)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/96/Maison_communale_Waterloo.jpg/330px-Maison_communale_Waterloo.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waterloo%2C_Belgium","wikidata":"Q179034"},{"title":"Waterloo (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Waterloo\" is a song recorded by Swedish pop music group ABBA, with music composed by Benny Andersson and Björn Ulvaeus and lyrics written by Stikkan Anderson. It is the first single of the group's second album of the same name, and their first under the Atlantic label in the United States. This was also the first single to be credited to the group performing under the name ABBA. The Swedish version of the single was backed with the Swedish version of \"Honey, Honey\", while the English version featured \"Watch Out\" on the B-side."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Waterloo_by_ABBA_US_single_side-A.png/330px-Waterloo_by_ABBA_US_single_side-A.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waterloo_(song)","wikidata":"Q32503"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Strategic_Situation_of_Western_Europe_1815.jpg/500px-Strategic_Situation_of_Western_Europe_1815.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Strategic_Situation_of_Western_Europe_1815.jpg/500px-Strategic_Situation_of_Western_Europe_1815.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The strategic situation in Western Europe in 1815: 250,000 Frenchmen faced about 850,000 allied soldiers on four fronts. In addition, Napoleon was forced to leave 20,000 men in Western France to reduce a royalist insurrection.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AStrategic_Situation_of_Western_Europe_1815.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project_%283x4_close_cropped%29.jpg/500px-Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project_%283x4_close_cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project_%283x4_close_cropped%29.jpg/500px-Jacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project_%283x4_close_cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJacques-Louis_David_-_The_Emperor_Napoleon_in_His_Study_at_the_Tuileries_-_Google_Art_Project_(3x4_close_cropped).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Sir_Arthur_Wellesley%2C_1st_Duke_of_Wellington.jpg/500px-Sir_Arthur_Wellesley%2C_1st_Duke_of_Wellington.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Sir_Arthur_Wellesley%2C_1st_Duke_of_Wellington.jpg/1280px-Sir_Arthur_Wellesley%2C_1st_Duke_of_Wellington.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Sir_Arthur_Wellesley%2C_1st_Duke_of_Wellington.jpg/500px-Sir_Arthur_Wellesley%2C_1st_Duke_of_Wellington.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Arthur Wellesley, 1st Duke of Wellington, commander of the Anglo-allied army, who had gained notable successes against the French in the Peninsular War","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASir_Arthur_Wellesley%2C_1st_Duke_of_Wellington.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/Field_Marshal_Prince_von_Blucher.jpg/500px-Field_Marshal_Prince_von_Blucher.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/